{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe is going to end. But of all the \npossible ends of the universe vacuum decay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would have to be the most thorough - because  it could totally rewrite the laws of physics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11600,
    "dDurationMs": 5279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today I hope to help you understand \nexactly how terrified you should be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems pretty lucky that the universe \nis how it is. Its just the right size,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has just the right expansion rate, and has just the right particle properties to allow stars and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets and people to exist. The habitability of our universe is largely defined by the properties  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the quantum fields that pervade all space. The quantum fields give rise to the particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that make up all matter and all forces - and \nif those quantum fields were a bit different,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "none of the familiar structures from atoms \nto galaxies - would be possible. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55120,
    "dDurationMs": 5236,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for most possible configurations of the quantum fields, no structures would exist at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60356,
    "dDurationMs": 4604,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately things are they way they are … although don’t get too cosy - there’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least one mechanism that could rewrite \nthe laws of physics across the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That mechanism is vacuum decay, and \nsome physicists think it’s inevitable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75440,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First to give you a crude picture - vacuum \ndecay looks like a bubble of annihilation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that expands at the speed of light, rewriting \nthe nature of the quantum fields as it passes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84240,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand whether and when this might happen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we first need to understand the \nquantum fields that it threatens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91680,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For that let’s try an analogy. Think of all space as being sort of springy at every point. As a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simplistic example, imagine a rubber ring at each point. If you compress the ring in one direction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it bounces back and begins to oscillate around its equilibrium shape. But each ring is also connected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to its neighboring rings, and so it can transfer its oscillations, causing a wave to propagate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through space. And there are other ways for the rings to oscillate - it has different vibrational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121280,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modes. For example it could twist back and forth on different axes, or deform or more complex ways.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127520,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can think of each quantum field as a set of these modes of oscillation. And each quantum field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133080,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- each type of oscillation - has a corresponding particle - that’s the oscillation itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like the deformed ring, a quantum field \nwants to return to its equilibrium position.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That position is the field value where the energy is minimized. Physicists like to represent this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by plotting the energy in the \nquantum field versus field value.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It takes more energy to get further away \nfrom the minimum - the equilibrium point,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158400,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just like it takes more energy if \nyou want to deform the rubber ring.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another nice physical analogy is a ball rolling in a dip. The ball will roll up and down,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oscillating around the equilibrium, and the height it reaches depends on how much energy it has."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For most quantum fields, the minimum \nenergy is where the field value is zero.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, for an electromagnetic wave \n- a photon - the electric and magnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields rise and fall between positive and \nnegative values, but the average field value  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is zero. And after the photon passes the \nelectromagnetic field goes back to zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s one quantum field that breaks these rules. That’s the Higgs field. The minimum energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197040,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state of the Higgs field is not where the field strength is zero. Instead, the Higgs field likes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203200,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to settle into an equilibrium value where it \nhas a real, positive value. This means that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207600,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entire universe is filled with this soup of \nHiggsiness. Most elementary particles that have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass gain their mass due to interactions with this ubiquitous field. I go into all the detail of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass-granting power of the Higgs mechanism in a previous video. For today, all you need to know is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that there's this one quantum field that has a minimum energy value where the field strength is non-zero - and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that this also means that the energy carried by the Higgs field is non-zero, even at this minimum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that's where the trouble starts. The Higgs field may have yet another weird property.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may have more than one possible minimum value. This would look like multiple dips in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our graph of energy versus field strength. \nA quantum field with multiple minima  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like this will tend to find its way into one \nof these dips and get stuck there. Just like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a ball rolling on an undulating surface. \nIn order to move between dips, the ball,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the quantum field, needs to gain enough \nenergy to overcome this barrier between them.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That could happen, say, in an extreme energy environment like the big bang or near a black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276800,
    "dDurationMs": 5721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole or in a sufficiently large particle accelerator. But there’s another to make this jump."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282521,
    "dDurationMs": 4679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Heisenberg uncertainty principle tells \nus that there’s a fundamental uncertainty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in - well, pretty much everything, but certainly in the value of a quantum field. This results in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuations in the field strength that \ncan cause it to spontaneously shift,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and perhaps find itself stuck in an adjacent \ndip. We call this quantum tunneling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what does this mean for the Higgs field? Well some theorists do believe that the Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field has at least two minima, and those minima should have different energy values. There’s a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true minimum - the true lowest energy, or what we call the true vacuum. This is where the field would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prefer to spend its time given the choice. And then there’s another local minimum with a higher  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324960,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy - what we call that a false vacuum. The false vacuum is a so-called metastable state - it seems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stable as long as the field doesn’t learn about the more stable, lower energy state. Somewhat  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worryingly, we don’t know which of these minima our universe’s Higgs field is in right now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343360,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s think about the potentially \ncatastrophic consequences of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this. Firstly, if the Higgs field is in the \ntrue minimum then no big deal. Even if the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field at one location of space tunneled \nor was excited into the false minimum,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357920,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would quickly find its way back to the true minimum for reasons I’ll get to. But what if the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361840,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire universe is in the false vacuum? Then a tunneling event would be a little more dramatic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine a universe filled with the Higgs field in a false vacuum. At a single point in space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a quantum tunneling event drops the field into the true vacuum. This creates a tiny bubble  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is at an energetically favorable \nstate compared to the surroundings.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The interior of the bubble immediately \ntries to drag the surrounding Higgs field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down with it. This happens because quantum fields are connected and tug at their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "adjacent points across space, which is why \ntheir oscillations propagate as particles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this isn’t necessarily an instant disaster. The interior of the bubble wants to expand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the shell of the bubble is actually in \nan energetically unfavorable state - it’s in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this unpleasant transitional state between \nthe two minima of the Higgs field. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415600,
    "dDurationMs": 6262,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bubble wall experiences a sort of surface tension that tries to collapse the bubble again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421862,
    "dDurationMs": 4698,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Things get awkward if the initial bubble \nexceeds a certain size. The bigger the bubble,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the more its interior wants to expand into its surroundings. But you also have more bubble surface,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so more surface tension to resist that \nexpansion. But the interior of the bubble  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increases with the cube of the radius, while \nthe surface increases only with radius squared.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443040,
    "dDurationMs": 5713,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means that above a certain size, the \nhunger of the bubble interior dominates.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448753,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The bubble grows, and that growth very quickly approaches the speed of light. Once it gets going,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454833,
    "dDurationMs": 4447,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bubble is unstoppable, and will drag \nthe Higgs field through the entire universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459280,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down into the true vacuum. This is vacuum decay. It’s a phase transition of the quantum fields.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467120,
    "dDurationMs": 5018,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact it has a lot of similarities with the sort of phase transitions that you get in matter.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472138,
    "dDurationMs": 5382,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, when water boils - it undergoes a phase transition to water vapor - that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phase transition also starts in small bubbles that grow into its surroundings. The formation of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these phase-transition bubbles in water or in a quantum field is called bubble nucleation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488720,
    "dDurationMs": 4899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, let’s review the horrors that \nwould result from vacuum decay.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493619,
    "dDurationMs": 5021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, everything gets fried. The energy \nreleased in the decay of the Higgs field fills  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the expanding bubble with a hot soup of energetic \nparticles. It’s similar to how the energy held in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the latent heat of boiling water is released \ninto the kinetic energy of gas molecules."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s not the worst of it. As I mentioned, the Higgs field gives elementary particles their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "masses. Those masses depend on the energy in the field - the so-called vacuum expectation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520720,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "value. Drop the energy in the field and you \nreduce the masses of the elementary particles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ability for stars to form and undergo \nnuclear fusion, and the ability for chemistry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530080,
    "dDurationMs": 5713,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to work as it does, depend sensitively on \nthe properties of the elementary particles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535793,
    "dDurationMs": 5087,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Life and structure could not exist as we know it, and may not exist at all. I should also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540880,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "add that there are other possible types \nof vacuum decay beyond Higgs field decay.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, there may be weird fields within string theory that exist in false vacuum states.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The decay of those could rewrite the \nlaws of physics in far more drastic ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This all sounds moderately bad. But can this actually happen? First let’s ask whether we really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562000,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are living in one of these nasty false \nvacuums - then we’ll talk about whether  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that vacuum might decay. Assuming the Higgs field really does have multiple minima, the question becomes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571040,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which minimum are we in? The true one or a false one? It turns out that the theorists aren’t sure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578640,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can determine the shape of the Higgs field with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580720,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "precise measurements of the particles \nthat gain their mass from the Higgs.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most important are the Higgs particle \nitself as well as the top quark, which is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most massive elementary particle. And our measurements of these masses tell us that … we are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably in the false vacuum - but we’re actually very close to the boundary. We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MIGHT be in the true vacuum - which would be good, but it’s a bit more likely that we’re in the bad,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "false one. Further study of the Higgs particle and top quark should help us figure this out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so the next question is could the vacuum really decay? As long as decay is possible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s inevitable. At any instant in time there’s a tiny but real probability that a patch of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621520,
    "dDurationMs": 5716,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space will tunnel between the false and true vacuums. Wait long enough and it WILL happen.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627236,
    "dDurationMs": 4604,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Happily, there’s only a very small probability \nthat a bubble of true vacuum will randomly form  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s large enough to actually continue \ngrowing. Physicists have not converged on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636320,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a single timescale - they vary from roughly the current age of the universe to 10^hundreds times  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643280,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the age of the universe for a single such bubble to be likely to appear somewhere in our observable universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650560,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, somewhere between vaguely \nunlikely and staggeringly unlikely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654560,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before you get too comfortable, remember \nthere’s another way to hop between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy minima. If enough energy can be pumped into a patch of space, the Higgs field can hop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between minima without tunneling. This would be like dropping specks of dust into superheated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669120,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water - dust provides sites for bubble nucleation, and the entire body of water rapidly evaporating. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there was some fear that our giant particle \ncolliders like the LHC might nucleate a vacuum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decay bubble. But that was unfounded. After all, Earth is constantly being bombarded by cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rays of much, much higher energy than the LHC can produce, and no annihilation just yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694480,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Overall, it’s very unlikely \nthat a vacuum decay bubble  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will reach us in the lifespan of our species, \nlet alone your own lifetime. Of course, in an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinitely or sufficiently large universe then vacuum decay has definitely started somewhere.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as long as we’re far enough away we’re safe. If the vacuum decay starts beyond several billion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light years, the accelerating expansion of the universe will throw us away from it faster than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble of annihilation can grow, even though that growth is at nearly the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this leads to one more comforting fact. If the vacuum does decay inside our cosmic horizon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ll never see it coming. Because no light \ncan outpace the expanding bubble to warn us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its approach. One moment you might \nbe watching your favorite YouTube show,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743440,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the next … well, there would be \nno next. Who knows - perhaps the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble edge has already swallowed your \ncomputer screen a nanosecond ago. …  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I guess not. Good. So, just for now let’s \nenjoy whatever time we have left - perhaps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757760,
    "dDurationMs": 7712,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mere billions of years - before vacuum decay swallows this fragile and metastable space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
